# Hybrid Record Classifier

A configurable text-classification pipeline that assigns records to a user-defined taxonomy. It combines transparent rule-based matching (names, phrases, keywords, hierarchical codes) with a zero-shot language-model fallback, and also extracts named entities from each record. Every prediction comes with a confidence score that reflects which stage made the decision.

## How it works

For each record the classifier tries the following stages in order and stops at the first match:

| Stage | Evidence | Confidence |
|---|---|---|
| 1 | Taxonomy match in the record **name** | highest |
| 2 | Taxonomy match in the **description** (explicit class phrase, then first sentence) | high |
| 3 | Structured **code** lookup (optional metadata rule) | high |
| 4 | **Zero-shot classification** (NLI model) over the combined text | model score |

The combined text used by the model stages is built from the record fields, led by any "*name* is a/an ..." sentences and the code association, so the strongest evidence appears first.

Named entities (type, text, score) are extracted with a token-classification model and kept when the score is at least `MIN_NER_CONFIDENCE`.

## Repository structure

```
.
├── classifier.py       # pipeline and configuration
├── requirements.txt
└── README.md
```

## Input data

| File | Required columns |
|---|---|
| `records.csv` | `record_id`, `name`, `hierarchy_code`, `function`, `description`, `details`, `tags` (a field can be left empty; the text is built from the columns listed in `INPUT_COLUMNS`) |
| `eligible_ids.csv` | `record_id` (only these records are classified) |

## Output

`classification_results.csv`: all input columns plus

- `predicted_class`: the assigned class,
- `confidence`: the confidence of the stage that decided,
- `entities`: extracted entities, stored as a string.

## Configuration

All settings are in the `CONFIG` section of `classifier.py`:

- `CLASS_KEYWORDS`: the taxonomy, as class label -> keywords. `CANDIDATE_LABELS` for the zero-shot stage is derived from it.
- `CODE_ASSOCIATIONS` and `CODE_PREFIX_LEN`: optional rule mapping code prefixes to classes.
- `PROTECTED_CLASSES`: classes whose first-sentence matches are only accepted when the keyword is also in the name or the sentence reads "... is *keyword*". Use it for classes with ambiguous keywords.
- `NER_MODEL_NAME` and `ZSC_MODEL_NAME`: any Hugging Face token-classification and zero-shot-classification models.
- `MIN_NER_CONFIDENCE` and the per-stage confidence values.

## Adapting to your case

`classifier.py` is a template and a guide, not a ready-to-run solution. Placeholders are marked `ADAPT` in the code. For each use case:

- insert the proper input data and column names,
- define the taxonomy (`Class A-C` and the keywords are placeholders),
- add, remove or reorder the rules in `infer_class` to suit the data (for example, drop the code rule if there is no code field).

## Getting started

```bash
git clone <repo-url>
cd <repo-folder>
python -m venv .venv
source .venv/bin/activate        # Windows: .venv\Scripts\activate
pip install -r requirements.txt
python classifier.py
```

Models are downloaded from the Hugging Face Hub on first run.

## Notes

- Rule-based stages run before the models, so most records never reach the zero-shot stage, which keeps runs fast and results explainable.
- Stage order encodes reliability. Reorder or remove stages in `infer_class` to suit your data.
- Records with no usable text and no rule match get an empty class.

